Easy Screen

Share a browser tab or window directly with other people, with no account, no install and no server of your own. Several people can share in the same room, with the host's permission. The whole app is a single HTML file.

Live site: https://cross-creative.github.io/easy_screen/


How it works

Video travels directly from the host's browser to each viewer's browser (peer-to-peer, using WebRTC). A public PeerJS server (https://peerjs.com) is used only to help browsers find each other. No video passes through it, and nothing is stored.


Quick start

Sharing

1. Open the site on a computer.
2. Choose a nickname, pick a quality level and decide whether to share audio.
3. Click "Choose a tab or window" and select what to share.
4. Send the 6-character room code, or the link, to your viewers.

You can also click "Create room without sharing (BETA)" to open the room first and share later.

After choosing the tab or window (or right away, when creating a room without sharing), you pick the room type. Public is the default: anyone with the link or code can join. Private: you get a notice with the person's nickname and accept or decline each one.

Watching

1. Open the link, or choose a nickname, type the room code on the home page and click "Join".
2. If the video starts muted because of browser autoplay rules, click "Unmute".
3. To share your own screen, click "Ask to share". When the host allows it, click "Share".


Features

Room codes: each host gets a random 6-character code, plus a shareable link that joins the room directly.

Nickname required to host or join. If the nickname is already taken, a dialog asks for another one instead of removing you from the room. The nickname is saved as you type and shown again after you leave a room. Opening a room link joins the room right away when a nickname is saved. Without one, you type the nickname and press Enter or click Join, and the code is already filled in.

The viewer sees "Host: name" at the top, and the host sees a viewer counter like "3 👁".

Chat: users can chat with each other. You are responsible for what you say, and the host can mute you, delete your messages or kick you.

Host moderation: mute, kick and delete messages from the people panel.

Temporary ban after a kick: 5 minutes by viewer identifier and 2 minutes by IP address.

Automatic reconnection: if a viewer's connection drops, the site retries up to 6 times with increasing delays and keeps the chat on screen.

Tab or window sharing with optional audio.

Multi-sharing: viewers ask to share and the host allows, denies or revokes. Up to 4 people share at the same time, host included.

Grid with every shared screen. Click a screen to see only that one, and use the button in its top right corner to go back to all screens. Fullscreen then shows only that screen.

Audio tab: when more than one person is sharing, the options panel shows a volume slider and a mute button for each shared screen. It only affects your own device.

Public and private rooms: in a private room the host accepts or declines each person who tries to join. Requests expire after 90 seconds, and people who were accepted can reconnect without asking again.

Quality levels from 480p up to QHD (1440p), adjustable while streaming.

Leave to menu: the host can go to the menu while the room stays open for 8 minutes, with a countdown shown to everyone, and come back with "Back to the room". Starting a new room closes the old one.

Warning for the host when upload bandwidth or CPU is overloaded, with a one-click option to lower the quality.

Estimated upload usage and a live viewer counter.

Up to 16 viewers per room.

Stop and resume sharing, or switch to another tab or window, without kicking anyone out of the room.

Options panel for the host, with a confirmation before ending the room.

One tab per viewer per room: duplicate tabs from the same browser are blocked.

Volume and mute controls for viewers, audio toggle for the host.

Fullscreen and Picture Mode (picture-in-picture) for both host and viewers, where the browser supports it. When more than one person is sharing, you choose which screen goes to Picture Mode.

Host recovery: if the host closes the tab, everyone in the room sees a notice with a 10-second countdown. If the host reopens the site in the same browser within that time, the home screen shows a "Back to the room" button with a countdown, and clicking it resumes the room with the same code, room type and permissions. Otherwise the person who joined the room first becomes the new host, keeping the same code and link. The chat, the ban list and any active sharing are lost in the handover.

English and Portuguese (Brazil), detected from the browser and remembered.

Light and dark theme, following the system setting.


Quality levels and upload

Each viewer receives a separate copy of the video, so the host's upload grows with the audience. These are approximate values per viewer:

Very light: 480p, 10 fps, about 0.3 Mbps
Economy: 720p, 15 fps, about 0.8 Mbps
Balanced: 720p, 24 fps, about 1.5 Mbps
High: 1080p, 30 fps, about 3 Mbps
QHD: 1440p, 60 fps, about 8 Mbps

Example: 4 viewers at Balanced need about 6 Mbps of upload. Anyone who shares uploads one copy to every other person in the room, so 4 people sharing in a room of 16 can need far more. The actual resolution also depends on the size of the shared tab or window.


Browser support

Desktop Chrome, Edge, Firefox and Safari: can share and can watch.
Android browsers: can only watch.
iOS browsers: can only watch.

Mobile browsers do not currently support screen capture, so on phones the site only lets you watch. Audio capture also depends on the browser: sharing a tab's audio works best in Chrome and Edge, and sharing a whole window's audio is limited.


Self-hosting

Self-hosting a copy requires prior written permission from the author (see License).

With permission, only index.html is required. The optional icone.png is used as the page icon, and the page works without it.

GitHub Pages

1. Put index.html in the root of a repository.
2. In the repository settings, open Pages and select the branch to publish.

Netlify

Drag the folder containing index.html into https://app.netlify.com/drop

Both serve the site over HTTPS, which browsers require for screen capture.


Limitations

Mesh topology: everyone who shares uploads one stream per person in the room. The limit is 16 viewers, but in practice it depends on the host's upload. It is best suited to small groups, roughly 3 to 6 people at higher quality levels.

Depends on the free public PeerJS server, which can be slow or unavailable.

Viewers behind very restrictive networks, such as some school or company networks, may fail to connect.

If the host loses internet or the computer freezes instead of closing the tab, the handover can take longer than 10 seconds.

The room type cannot be changed after the room is created. Creating a room without sharing is a beta feature.

Duplicate-tab detection only works within the same browser profile. Another browser, a private window or another device counts as a different viewer.

Bans after a kick are temporary and can be bypassed, for example with a VPN or a different network. The IP ban may also affect other people who share the same network.

If the host closes the tab, the room ends immediately. Joining another room as a viewer also closes your own room.

Automatic reconnection stops after 6 failed attempts, and the viewer then has to join again.


Privacy

No accounts, no analytics and no cookies.

The browser stores only your language choice, nickname and a random identifier used to detect duplicate tabs and to apply temporary bans. All of them stay on your device.

Because connections are peer-to-peer, the host and each viewer can see each other's IP address. Keep this in mind before sharing a room code publicly.

The host's browser reads each viewer's IP address only to apply a temporary ban after a kick. It is kept in memory, is never displayed and is discarded when the room ends.

Chat is not stored or recorded. It is cleared when the room ends.

Only share content you have the right to share. Streaming protected material, such as movies or series, may violate the terms of the service that provides it.


